@* macOS: the shaders in Metal.
Metal does not understand HLSL. Its shading language, MSL, is a dialect of C++14 with
vector and matrix types named like those of HLSL (|float3|, |float4x4|), so the
translation of <<file:shaders/pbr.hlsl>> is close to mechanical. The differences fall into
a few groups, which this part takes in order.

\begin{itemize}
\item \emph{Resources are parameters.} HLSL declares textures, samplers and constant
buffers as global variables bound to numbered registers (|register(t0)|). In MSL they are
parameters of the shader function, and the binding number is an attribute of the
parameter: |texture2d<float> colorMap [[texture(0)]]|.
\item \emph{Semantics become attributes.} The vertex inputs carry |[[attribute(n)]]|
instead of |POSITION|, |NORMAL|, \dots; the clip-space position is marked |[[position]]|
instead of |SV_Position|; other outputs need no names at all.
\item \emph{Shader stages are keywords.} A function preceded by |vertex| or |fragment| is
an entry point. (Metal says \emph{fragment} where Direct3D says \emph{pixel}.)
\item \emph{Different names for the same functions.} |mul(v, M)| becomes |M * v| (with
column vectors, \S\ref{macmath}), |lerp| becomes |mix|, |frac| becomes |fract|,
|SampleLevel(s, uv, l)| becomes |sample(s, uv, level(l))|, and |GetDimensions| becomes
|get_width| and |get_height|.
\end{itemize}

Everything else---and that includes all the mathematics---is unchanged.

<<file:mac/shaders/pbr.metal>>=
